<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ostpolitik</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1969–1974</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Neue Ostpolitik</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ostpolitik was the policy of reconciliation with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> pursued by Willy Brandt as West German foreign minister and then chancellor from 1969. Through the treaties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> in 1970 and the Basic Treaty of 1972 with East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> accepted the post-war borders in practice. Brandt received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1971.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ostpolitik.jpg" alt="Ostpolitik" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_B_145_Bild-F031406-0017,_Erfurt,_Treffen_Willy_Brandt_mit_Willi_Stoph.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
